[P — THE `Retrig` PILL IS A CONTROL ON EVERY TYPE x CHARACTER]
      🔴 fb425. `Retrig` IS A FRONT PILL AND IT WAS BIT-IDENTICALLY DEAD ON 3 OF 6
      TYPES. fireRetrig() reset ph_/res_/q_ — the per-voice LFO phase, which is read
      by procVoices' mod-0 branch and by procTwin AND BY NOTHING ELSE. `Twofold` runs
      a band-limited random walk (wk_), `Blur` a rotator plus its own walk
      (blurRot_/wkb_), `Bands` the crossover-grid rotator plus wkg_. Firing the pill
      on any of the three produced OUTPUT IDENTICAL TO THE BIT. The old §I click test
      toggled the pill — on Type 0 only — and a click test cannot see a control that
      does nothing, because doing nothing is the quietest thing a control can do.

      THE GATE. Two engines, same probe, same params. At t = 1.0 s one of them gets a
      single rising edge on `Retrig`. Then:
        1. BEFORE the edge the two outputs must be BIT-IDENTICAL — that proves the
           edge is the only difference, i.e. this is a controlled experiment.
        2. AFTER it they must not be, and the difference is measured on the OUTPUT
           with changeDist (log-band magnitude + correlation + field trajectory).
      THE ANCHOR IS A CONTROL OF THE SAME CLASS (fb417). A retrigger re-aligns the
      MODULATOR and nothing else, so the unit it is measured in is one quarter-turn
      of `Rate` — the other control on this device that moves the modulator and only
      the modulator — at the same FIFTH §F already defends for `Rate` and `Roam`. A
      quarter-turn of Mix is printed beside it for scale, and it is deliberately NOT
      the bar: 0.25 -> 0.50 wet is a doubling of the whole effect, a different class
      of event, and holding a phase re-alignment to a fifth of THAT would be a
      constant chosen to fail rather than a unit chosen to fit.
      A cell where the mechanism genuinely has no trajectory to restart is declared
      in the KNOWN-INERT ROSTER below — asserted size, reason per entry, and checked
      in reverse so a declaration cannot go stale.

